        "title": "Round 5 - The Drink That Did Not Kill",
        "narration": "The remaining brandy and the fragments of Ambrose's glass are compared. The decanter has no wolfsbane residue; the individual glass rim does. Constance drank from the same decanter and survived. Pouring the drinks is not the same as touching one rim during the blackout.\n\nThe doorway account describes a serpent-and-staff ring on the reaching hand. Ashgrove wears that physician's ring. The doorway account is recorded beside the glass findings. Its description of a ring should be tested against the poison evidence and the movements in the dark. The medium's script explains the false haunting. It does not explain the poisoned glass. The stolen candlestick explains Crane's doorway movement without making him an innocent witness by decree.\n\nKeep the earlier accusations beside these corrections. Ask which explanation fits the bottle, the shared drink and the single poisoned rim without changing any of those facts.\n\nThe host reads the supplied character accounts verbatim; players need no absent actor:\nDr. Silas Ashgrove: \"I know Hollis Crane noticed Eleanor's tonic bottle and the glass evidence. I see why he'd hide stolen silver, but that clean decanter leaves Ambrose's poisoned rim unexplained.\"\nHollis Crane: \"I learned Father Lucan Grey kept Eleanor's warning under confession's seal. She said medicine, not fever, would kill her. I understand silence, but I still want the tonic checked.\"\nLady Constance Ravenmoor: \"I heard Dr. Silas Ashgrove call it a weak heart despite recognizing poison symptoms. The clean decanter points to Ambrose's glass. With his old tonic and that scripted question, I'm not convinced.\"\nMadame Vesper Thorne: \"I saw Lady Constance Ravenmoor drink from that decanter and survive; poison was on Ambrose's glass alone. Her inheritance troubles me, but pouring isn't proof.\"\nFather Lucan Grey: \"I read Ambrose's paid script telling Madame Vesper Thorne to ask, \"Who gave me the tonic?\" I see a planned trap behind her hidden fraud, not proof of supernatural murder.\"\n\nFurther comparisons from the original twelve-character source:\nDr. Silas Ashgrove dismissed poisoning symptoms as a weak heart.\nHollis Crane compared the tonic bottle with the glass findings.\nLady Constance Ravenmoor survived brandy poured from the same decanter.\nAmbrose's script ordered Madame Vesper Thorne to ask who gave Eleanor the tonic.\nFather Lucan Grey kept Eleanor's warning under confession's seal.\nGideon Marsh held the codicil naming the signer of Eleanor's death certificate.\nBottles washed in Mother Agnes Morley's kitchen smelled of wolfsbane a year ago.\nTheodora \"Teddy\" Finch's photograph shows a snake-shaped gold ring near the glass.\nWren Hollow preserved Eleanor's diary about being watched while drinking.\nBeside Ivy Marchetti's concealed letters was Ambrose's warning about S.A.'s hands.\nCaptain Rourke Vane carried a gun, but Ambrose died of poisoning.\nProfessor Edmund Blackwood concealed the book theft but supplied poison knowledge.",
