        "narration": "Eleanor's preserved correspondence describes numb lips after her nightly medicine and plants dying when she poured it into their pots. Her diary says, \"S. knows that I know. He watches me drink every night.\" With the letters is an account of false prescriptions and two patients who died. Eleanor had intended to tell Ambrose. Ashgrove signed her death certificate as fever.\n\nAmbrose's written instructions to the medium are found: ask, \"Who gave me the tonic?\" He paid for a trap, not a genuine spirit. A note from his coat says, \"Watch S.A.'s hands when the lights return.\" The withheld codicil directs attention to the signer of Eleanor's death certificate. The records are now on the table for these guests to compare.\n\nNow read the new clues. Documents supply a reason to fear this gathering. The final task is still to connect that fear to the movements around Ambrose's glass.\n\nThe host reads the supplied character accounts verbatim; players need no absent actor:\nDr. Silas Ashgrove: \"I heard Lady Constance Ravenmoor wish Ambrose would join Eleanor during their dinner argument. With her gambling debts and those poured drinks, I can't dismiss that resentment.\"\nHollis Crane: \"I read Dr. Silas Ashgrove's fever certificate, but Eleanor's discarded tonic numbed her lips and killed plants. His bottle initials worry me. I want that medicine explained.\"\nLady Constance Ravenmoor: \"I learned Madame Vesper Thorne was paid triple for Ambrose's script and controlled the bellows. With exposure threatened too, I can't call that staged darkness harmless entertainment.\"\nMadame Vesper Thorne: \"I learned Father Lucan Grey heard Eleanor say medicine, not fever, would kill her. He stayed silent when Ambrose demanded justice. I wonder whether he's protecting a confession or a person.\"\nFather Lucan Grey: \"I learned Hollis Crane returned to pocket a candlestick in the seance room. That puts him in the doorway during darkness. I can't ignore movement while Ambrose's glass was within reach.\"\n\nFurther comparisons from the original twelve-character source:\nDr. Silas Ashgrove certified fever despite Eleanor's numb lips and dying plants.\nHollis Crane's candlestick theft places him in the doorway during darkness.\nLady Constance Ravenmoor wished Ambrose would join Eleanor during their dinner argument.\nAmbrose paid Madame Vesper Thorne to follow his script and control the bellows.\nFather Lucan Grey heard Eleanor say medicine, not fever, would kill her.\nAmbrose instructed Gideon Marsh to open the codicil if he died.\nMother Agnes Morley recognized the symptoms and recalled a doctor's bag and lethal-dose question.\nTheodora \"Teddy\" Finch concealed a dismissal for invented reporting.\nWren Hollow knew Eleanor's discarded tonic killed the room's plants.\nIvy Marchetti threatened regret after Ambrose ended their affair.\nCaptain Rourke Vane knew wolfsbane could kill and smelled crushed plants.\nProfessor Edmund Blackwood carried a book describing poison resembling heart failure.",
