        "narration": "Place the evidence in order. Before tonight, Eleanor recorded harm from medicine and threatened to expose the prescription accounts. Tonight Ambrose brought the bottle and hired the performance to ask about it. During the blackout, a chair scraped, a ringed hand reached toward his glass, and he died. The poison was on that glass alone.\n\nThe final comparison adds the glove observation: Ashgrove removed his gloves before the seance and replaced them when the lights returned. It is a witness detail to test against the ring account, not a confession. His immediate weak-heart explanation must also be weighed against the poison symptoms, handwriting and soil.\n\nRead every final clue. Other guests have debts and wrongdoing, but do those explain both deaths and this method? Name a suspect from the evidence already spoken, not from a secret packet or a supernatural answer.\n\nThe host reads the supplied character accounts verbatim; players need no absent actor:\nDr. Silas Ashgrove: \"I know Madame Vesper Thorne hid a fraud. Ambrose knowingly hired it to ask about the tonic, but died before that question. I still wonder who poisoned his glass in her darkness.\"\nHollis Crane: \"I know Lady Constance Ravenmoor inherits, but her shared drink was harmless. Pouring isn't touching Ambrose's rim in darkness. Her account of Eleanor's numb lips makes me worry about that tonic.\"\nLady Constance Ravenmoor: \"I know Father Lucan Grey withheld a warning from before tonight's murder; it points to the tonic. Threatened exposure of his drinking doesn't explain the poisoned rim or ring to me.\"\nMadame Vesper Thorne: \"I place Hollis Crane in the doorway; the candlestick explains his presence, not both deaths. That serpent-and-staff ring description and poison on Ambrose's glass still need explaining to me.\"\nFather Lucan Grey: \"I recognize Dr. Silas Ashgrove's serpent-and-staff ring on that reaching hand; wolfsbane coats Ambrose's rim, not the decanter. Bottle and soil link both deaths. I doubt Dr. Silas Ashgrove's 'weak heart'; Eleanor's tonic question threatens him.\"\n\nFurther comparisons from the original twelve-character source:\nThe reaching hand wore Dr. Silas Ashgrove's serpent-and-staff ring.\nHollis Crane's doorway account describes the ring beside the glass.\nLady Constance Ravenmoor shared harmless brandy and reported Eleanor's numb lips.\nMadame Vesper Thorne staged Ambrose's trap, but he died before the tonic question.\nFather Lucan Grey's withheld warning predates tonight's murder.\nGideon Marsh's preserved codicil points to Eleanor's death certificate.\nMother Agnes Morley recognized the plant's smell on Eleanor's prescribed tonic bottles.\nTheodora \"Teddy\" Finch's image agrees with the independent doorway account.\nWren Hollow preserved the diary warning that someone watched Eleanor drink.\nIvy Marchetti's letters concealed Ambrose's warning to watch S.A.'s hands.\nCaptain Rourke Vane described the physician beside Ambrose when the candles returned.\nProfessor Edmund Blackwood saw gloves removed before the seance and replaced afterward.",
