        "title": "Round 2 - The Bottle and the Wire",
        "narration": "Three knocks sound beneath the table. A wire catches the candlelight. The spirit board spells TONIC, but the mechanism, not a ghost, moved it. Ambrose hired Vesper to stage a performance; the script has not yet been examined.\n\nA servant brings down Eleanor's old tonic bottle, kept at Ambrose's request for the constable. Its label bears S.A. in Ashgrove's handwriting. In the conservatory, monkshood stems have been freshly cut; soil leads toward the physician's chair. Monkshood is also called wolfsbane. Its poison can produce the symptoms seen on Ambrose.\n\nAccounts, letters and household complaints now expose the disputes surrounding tonight: Constance's gambling debt, Crane's stolen silver, and Vesper's threatened exposure as a fraud. The bottle links the present investigation to Eleanor's death a year ago. It does not yet show how tonight's poison reached the victim.\n\nThe host reads the supplied character accounts verbatim; players need no absent actor:\nDr. Silas Ashgrove: \"I learned Eleanor told Father Lucan Grey she feared her medicine; he never warned us. His silence protected someone while she worsened. I'm worried.\"\nHollis Crane: \"I found a wire beneath the table linking Madame Vesper Thorne to those spirit knocks. Ambrose threatened to expose her fraud. I wonder what she'd do to silence him.\"\nLady Constance Ravenmoor: \"I see stolen silver linking Hollis Crane to secret dealings outside the manor. He knows the drinks, and his livelihood's threatened. After that tonic discovery, I'm uneasy.\"\nMadame Vesper Thorne: \"I found initials on Eleanor's old tonic bottle. I recognize S.A. in Dr. Silas Ashgrove's handwriting; cut-monkshood soil leads toward his chair. I wonder if it's the same poison twice.\"\nFather Lucan Grey: \"I learned Lady Constance Ravenmoor hid gambling debts; Ambrose refused her money at dinner. Her inheritance would settle them. I wonder whether pouring those drinks was more than hospitality.\"\n\nFurther comparisons from the original twelve-character source:\nEleanor's tonic bottle bears Dr. Silas Ashgrove's handwriting and initials.\nStolen silver connects Hollis Crane to concealed dealings outside the manor.\nLady Constance Ravenmoor hid gambling debts that her inheritance would settle.\nA wire connects Madame Vesper Thorne to the fraudulent spirit knocks.\nFather Lucan Grey withheld Eleanor's fear of her medicine.\nUnexplained estate withdrawals point to Gideon Marsh.\nCut monkshood grew in the garden tended by Mother Agnes Morley.\nTheodora \"Teddy\" Finch kept the photograph while it developed.\nWren Hollow preserved the room where Eleanor had taken her tonic.\nIvy Marchetti concealed love letters taken from the body.\nCaptain Rourke Vane noticed a sharp green smell during the blackout.\nProfessor Edmund Blackwood hid a grimoire describing wolfsbane poisoning.",
